package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setBloomburrow = StorySection(
    "blb", "Bloomburrow",
    "In a valley where a strawberry can feed half a town and a dandelion stands taller than a house, a mouse who bakes for a living picks up an old sword and goes to find out why the seasons have gone mad.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-valley", "Valley",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-tiny-tails", "Tiny Tails, Big World",
                    listOf(
                        "Bloomburrow came out on August 2, 2024, under the set code BLB, the hundred and first expansion in the game's history. It is the first set of the Dragonstorm arc. In the order of the story it comes after the outlaws of [Thunder Junction](thunder-junction) and before the haunted House of [Duskmourn](duskmourn), in the restless years after the Phyrexian invasion when [the Omenpaths](the-omenpaths) had opened doors between worlds and very few planeswalkers were left to use the old ones.",
                        "It was also something Magic had never tried. Bloomburrow is a plane with no humans on it at all, and no human has ever set foot there. Its people are animals, mice and rabbits and frogs and bats and a dozen kinds more, who wear clothes, bake cakes, raise families, argue over dinner and weave magic. They are not the tall animal people of other worlds, the leonin or the aven or the nezumi. They are the size of the animals they are. A mouse is mouse sized. A badger can stand three times as tall as a mouse. A blade of grass is a tree and a boulder is a mountain, and one ripe strawberry is enough to feed half a village.",
                        "The plane keeps its shape on anyone who comes. A planeswalker who arrives is changed into an animal, and the kind of animal seems to depend on the creatures native to the world they came from. Nobody on Bloomburrow knows why. It might be a natural enchantment, an old artifact, a mighty spell, a Calamity Beast with power over the whole plane, or the aftermath of something divine. Whether a traveller who walks in through an Omenpath is changed the same way is not known either. Animalfolk who leave keep their own forms, though they may change in size.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-five-countries", "Five Kinds of Country",
                    listOf(
                        "Most of the set happens in Valley, a small temperate stretch of land inside the larger plane. It holds five kinds of country. The meadows are seas of amber grass and wildflowers, with tall pampas and yarrow making a canopy for the folk who walk beneath, and farms woven gently through them. Tomatoes, peppers and spring onions crowd the fields in spring and summer, and when Valley turns cold the harvest becomes cabbage, radishes and carrots. Dandelion seeds drift through the air like snow.",
                        "The ponds are fed by rain and do not touch the river. Lily pads give the water folk somewhere to rest between dives, and a tired traveller who sees one knows a village is close. The Long River runs the whole length of Valley from somewhere above the forests down to the marshes and beyond, and fish swim it all the way. The marshes are where its tributaries go slow and silty, and there the ratfolk tunnel into the banks, mussels and clams spit muck at anyone careless, and snail shells and old bones wash up along the shore.",
                        "The hills are craggy and sparse, warm for the lizardfolk who bask on their rocks, and so tall that climbing a single boulder can take days and is worth boasting about afterwards. The thickets are old forest, mossy and wrapped in ivy, with squat round oaks hundreds of seasons old and trees large enough to hold whole cities. Beyond the farms and villages lies everything wild, which the folk call the Brambles. Five uncommon lands in the set, {Lupinflower Village}, {Lilypad Village}, {Mudflat Village}, {Rockface Village} and {Oakhollow Village}, each show a settlement where four of Valley's peoples live together.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Interrupting a rabbitfolk dinner is a most grievous offense. If you must do so, bring gifts for the little ones, or bring a shield.",
                        "Giddy's Guide to Valley, Head of the Homestead",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-towns-and-cities", "Towns and Cities",
                    listOf(
                        "{Three Tree City} is the largest place in Valley, built where the forest meets the Long River in the arms of three ancient trees, an oak, a sycamore and a willow, grown together in a way no one can explain. Its founders hoped for harmony, and in places that meant careful terraces of graceful buildings and paths of bracket fungus winding up the trunks, while elsewhere it meant a cheerful jumble of carved wood, painted clay, feathers, beads and embroidery. Doorways come in every size so the smallest and largest folk can share a street. At the top is the Crown, a promenade where all of Valley can be seen. At the heart is the Quilted District, under a vast quilted banner with a square from every kind of animalfolk that lives in or visits the city. Along the water under the willow's hanging tendrils lie the Docklands, a sprawl of rafts and piers where most of the city's trade comes in.",
                        "{Fountainport} rises from Valley's largest pond as a three tiered fountain of carved soapstone, with a sculpted lily at the top and a spire of water held up by weaving above the throne room. Glass elevators ride on pools of water from tier to tier, slides and waterfalls connect the levels, and at the base lies the rowdy Tadpool Harbor District, where trade happens out of sight of the king. The city belongs mostly to frogfolk and other water dwellers, and celebrations there never seem to stop.",
                        "Smaller places make up most of Valley. Goodhill has roofs of white petals, woven grass walls, painted clay burrows, a tall windmill and batfolk homes on long poles. Haymeadow lies at the south end of the main road. Valley has no single ruler. Villages have mayors or councils, and a city may have a king, usually elected, who serves only a few seasons. Magic is called weaving, and almost anyone can learn a little of it to light a hearth or patch a roof.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-the-brambles", "The Brambles",
                    listOf(
                        "Off the beaten paths, the Brambles hold the sacred and dangerous places of Valley. The raccoonfolk keep a hoard of relics with no obvious use in the Root Maze, a labyrinth of roots under a woven canopy. The lizardfolk revere the Ever-Burning Oak, split by fire and lightning in an ancient war and still smouldering, and young lizards journey there to take an ember from its heart with their bare hands. When a lizard's ember finally goes out, they know their own end is near. On the highest hill stands the Cliff of Heroes, where the sun is said never to set and where bands of mousefolk climb a single great boulder to prove their courage and their teamwork. The Forest of Iron is a deep crag of petrified ash trees whose bark rusts and whose oldest cores are pure steel.",
                        "Darker places lie further out. The Calamity Graveyard is a vast pit of tar and the bones of slain Calamity Beasts, where squirrelfolk come to worship the cycle of life and death and to gather bones for necromancy. Mushroom Valley grows huge fungi over a deep leyline. The ratfolk keep their Knowledge Ossuaries in caverns below the marshes, and deep underground they revere the Fell Comet, a floating stone whose gravity has frozen the water around it into a basin where young rats prove their cunning.",
                        "The batfolk read the sky in the Crater of the Cosmos, an obsidian bowl left by some event eons old where condensed water turns the walls into a mirror of the stars, and at the Lighttrap Spires an elder trades their sight for visions of realms beyond. The rabbitfolk walk a winding pilgrimage path through the Flower Garden that no flower may ever be harmed by. Birdfolk fly the Plumecreed to the Tip of the World, a mountain no one else dares climb. And at the far edge of Valley the Burning Bog smoulders forever, where the known lands end.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Water surges in a mysterious crater created eons ago, revered by batfolk as a nexus for the cosmos.",
                        "Sol Ring, Bloomburrow Commander",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Bloomburrow, Parts 1, 2 and 3, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Bloomburrow plane"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-lily-of-valley", "Lily of Valley",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-season-of-chaos", "The Season of Chaos",
                    listOf(
                        "Long ago, before the land was called Valley and before anyone understood how the great seasons turned, the plane ran wild with magic. The big ate the small and the strong preyed on the weak, and the strongest of all were the Calamity Beasts, towering wordless creatures whose very presence brought blizzards, floods, droughts and blight. Against them nobody could stand alone. Those who banded together to survive came to call themselves animalfolk, and they lived underground, building their cultures and their friendships in the tunnels while the world above them went to ruin.",
                        "Some were not content to live in the dark. A band formed of many kinds of animalfolk, sworn to win back a free life above ground, and under a holly bush heavy with berries they spoke a pact that was itself magic, binding them to each other and to the land until a home and a peace were secured. The holly leaf became their sign, sewn into their clothes and painted on their shields. They are remembered as the Order of the Holly Leaf, though to most folk now they are only a story.",
                        "The pact did something more. The land became a wellspring they could draw on. They could reshape the earth, call lightning down on their foes, raise the dead and even bend time. The greatest of them were called weavers and their art was called weaving, words and deeds as threads that brought power into being. It was the birth of every magic that Valley still knows.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-war-of-cinders", "The War of Cinders",
                    listOf(
                        "Weaving was not enough to break the Calamities. For two generations the War of Cinders raged across Valley. The animalfolk slew the weakest beasts when they could, and then the greatest Calamity of all appeared, Wildsear, the Season of Flames, the Primordial Fire, the Scouring Maw that burns through the seams of this plane and the next. Its firestorms erased everything the folk had won. The great oak was split by fire and lightning and smoulders still. The animalfolk were driven back underground and dug deeper and deeper as the flames above grew hotter.",
                        "Under those fires a mouse named Lily was born. She was a humble farmer who grew up pruning the giant mushrooms in the tunnels, and she moved with her family each time their burrows collapsed, until they were driven into the heart of Valley with death on every side. As the Calamities closed in, Lily stood and reached into the old vow, and something woke in her, born of her love for her friends, her family and the world she tended. It was the Seed Flame, a renewing power even the Calamity Beasts could not overcome.",
                        "The Seed Flame stripped Wildsear of much of its power and turned Lily into a radiant sun, a mouse with the might of nature itself. For three days her light burned across Valley and drove the predators back, weakening them all. In the last battle she fought Wildsear once more, broke off one of its teeth and drove it beyond the Brambles. From that tooth she made the sword Cragflame. The art of {Season of the Bold} shows her standing against the wildfire wolf, and the Seed Flame gave her the name she is also known by, Lily Emberseed.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "In hand, a sword to slay the frightful beast; in heart, these words to soothe our frightened kin.",
                        "Lily and the Ember Seed, Thistledown Players",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-calamities-within", "Calamities Within",
                    listOf(
                        "Peace came, and Valley began, small as a sapling, at the place that would become Three Tree City. It did not last as it was. In the seasons after Lily saved them the animalfolk grew indulgent, and weaving grew too strong. Quarrels that should have been small turned into battles that shook the world. The surging magic began to backlash on its users, and weavers became monsters made of nothing but rage and raw power, rampaging beyond anyone's ability to stop. Some began to wonder whether this was where the Calamity Beasts had come from in the first place. At the same time the seasons, cruel but at least regular while the beasts roamed, fell into chaos, a snowy morning giving way to a hurricane by dusk.",
                        "So the heroes sealed their greatest knowledge away. Weaving survived, but only in small forms anyone could manage, and the great weavings were hidden somewhere deep in Valley, never to be taught again. Lily left too. She gave her armour and her sword to her descendants and walked out through the Brambles, and the stories say Cragflame waits for a true hearted hero to wake its primordial fire again.",
                        "With the great weavings gone, the Calamities came back, and the folk learned cleverer ways to live with them. Frogs learned to predict their paths and otters chased their storms. Rats kept the knowledge. Lizards learned to make great spells from small magic. Squirrels came to worship the beasts and raise the dead from their bones. Raccoons and birds scouted and marked their lairs, bats sought protection in the stars, rabbits fed everyone, and the little mice stood in the way and led the beasts off from the villages. Each people's gift grew out of that time, and so did Valley as it is now.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Bloomburrow, Part 1, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Bloomburrow, Episode 2: An Expected Party, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lily Emberseed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Bloomburrow plane"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-calamity-beasts", "The Calamity Beasts",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-living-seasons", "Living Seasons",
                    listOf(
                        "Bloomburrow has no natural seasons. On other worlds the year turns because the world tilts toward its sun and away again. In Valley the weather is never a coincidence. A season begins when a Calamity Beast comes, and ends when it goes. Winter arrives because the Blizzard Elk is walking through Valley. Summer is heralded by the Sun Hawk scorching the air. Spring is Beza. The animalfolk name each season for the beast that brings it, and while some come every year as regular as a calendar, others come once in a generation and are remembered for the rest of everyone's lives.",
                        "The beasts are huge, wordless and not sapient. They are elementals, living natural disasters, and they are every bit as much a part of nature as the animalfolk are. Wherever one goes the world changes. Leaves wither, hills crack and scorch, or crops bloom in an instant. Some of that passes with the beast, and some stays as craters, poisoned soil or new ponds. Not all of them are hunters. Some are simply so big that their passing is a threat. The folk owe their good harvests to the beasts as much as they blame their lean ones on them.",
                        "The animalfolk sort them in two. The greatest Calamities are unique and have names, and no two of them are alike. The lesser ones are seen more often and their powers reach less far, a beast that makes the air hot rather than setting the forest alight, or that freezes the water underfoot rather than the whole sky. To a mouse the difference can be the difference between life and death.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The living seasons come to grow, to burn, to wither, and to freeze. As natural as it is for the Calamity Beasts to live, it is just as natural for them to die.",
                        "Repel Calamity",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-great-calamities", "The Great Calamities",
                    listOf(
                        "{Maha, Its Feathers Night} is the Night Owl, also called the Great Night Owl and the Eclipse Owl, and its coming is the Season of Long Night. Its wingspan is as broad as the branches of an oak, deep blue and violet with black barred feathers, its eyes and beak lit with turquoise magic. Where it flies, night follows, cutting across a bright sky like scissors through cloth to show the stars beneath. It moves in perfect silence, and the only sign of its passing is the ruin left behind. {Eluge, the Shoreless Sea} is the Flood Gar, an enormous fish whose arrival is the Season of Deep Waters, when ponds that could always be seen across seem to have no far shore.",
                        "{Beza, the Bounding Spring} is the Spring Elk, so loved that feasts are thrown in its name. It is said never to have chased anyone, and folk have seen it fight off other Calamities. {Lumra, Bellow of the Woods} is a vast bear whose roar raises forests out of bare ground, dense and endless and impossible to find a way out of. {Ygra, Eater of All} is a cat whose coming is the Season of Salt, when fields and friends alike turn into pillars of salt and the beast stays hungry still.",
                        "Greatest of them in the old stories is {Wildsear, Scouring Maw}, the Wildfire Wolf and Season of Flames, whose tooth became Cragflame. Lily took the Seed Flame from it long ago, and it is a shadow of what it was, though still more than a match for any village. The five mythic beasts of the main set also come in a borderless showcase called Fieldnotes, styled as notes made by those who have studied them. Wildsear arrived in the Commander decks instead.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Its wingspan reaches from dusk to dawn.",
                        "Maha, Its Feathers Night",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-lesser-calamities", "Lesser Calamities",
                    listOf(
                        "The lesser Calamities have names too, though they are names for a kind rather than a single creature. The Sun Hawk, shown on {Pyreswipe Hawk}, heralds summer. The Blizzard Elk, shown on {Galewind Moose}, brings winter. The Blight Snake, shown on {Rottenmouth Viper}, makes nature rot around it, and fighting it is said to be like fighting starvation itself. The Drought Cat, shown on {Sunspine Lynx}, dries the land. These are the beasts the mayor of Goodhill meant when he told a worried crowd that the unpredictable ones did not trouble the little folk of Valley any more.",
                        "A cycle of uncommons gives one heavy beast to each colour, {Driftgloom Coyote} in white, {Eddymurk Crab} in blue, {Huskburster Swarm} in black, {Quaketusk Boar} in red and the moose in green. Each is the most expensive card of its colour in the set. Others roam the green pages, among them {Fecund Greenshell}, a turtle, and {Hivespine Wolverine}. Only one kind of creature shows up as both animalfolk and beast. There are birdfolk, and there are Calamity birds, and nothing else crosses that line.",
                        "One thing that looks like a Calamity is not one. {Dragonhawk, Fate's Tempest} sits in the same mythic cycle as the great beasts, but the story is plain that the folk of Valley do not count it among them. It is a bird with four wings, the tips webbed like a bat's, and a crest like a lizard's, and it brings storms unlike any storm Valley knows. The lorekeepers had no record of anything like it. A stranger who saw it thought it was a dragon from another world, changed by the plane like everyone else who arrives.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "There are no words in Valley to describe this new Calamity. It might not even be one at all. It brings storms with it, though nothing resembling the storms of Valley.",
                        "Bello, bard of the Brambles, The Legends of Bloomburrow",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Bloomburrow, Part 1, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Bloomburrow, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Calamity Beast"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-fields-and-skies", "Fields and Skies",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-birdfolk", "Birdfolk",
                    listOf(
                        "The birdfolk are the white and blue folk of Valley, raised to be idealists who stand up for the weak and never look away from injustice. They carry the mail, deliver aid and fly other folk where they need to go, and they think of themselves as natural leaders. In an adventuring party a bird will often declare itself in charge and open each day with a grand speech. They care a great deal about how they look, and in larger villages they gather in bathhouses called preeneries to groom and keep up appearances. At their best they are chivalrous and selfless. At their worst they are stubborn and patronizing.",
                        "Their most famous tradition is the Plumecreed, a dangerous flight across Valley to the Tip of the World, where the bird plants a wing feather at the peak and weaves an ancient spell before leaving. Those who come back are Windcrested, blessed with speed, and they wear a single dyed feather to show it. Birdfolk fight by diving from the sky with a lance and are clumsy when trapped on the ground. Their magic follows an ethic called the Wingbeat Way and is meant to help their allies. Their homes, called Perches, stand in trees or on stilts, and their doors are left open for anyone in danger. In the set, birds are the white and blue fliers deck, led by cards like {Kastral, the Windcrested} and {Salvation Swan}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Any fledgling can flap their wings. But a true windscout must soar!",
                        "Plumecreed Mentor",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-batfolk", "Batfolk",
                    listOf(
                        "The batfolk are Valley's night keepers, white and black, superstitious and kind and fiercely protective. Their communities are built on rigid roles and clear hierarchies drawn from the patterns of the moon and the stars. They believe in balance above everything, no night without day and no life without death, and because they keep watch alone through the dark while others sleep, they ask the day folk for a little of their life force to fuel that protection. Their ways of collecting it can be heavy handed, which is the set's white and black life drain theme in a sentence. Batfolk who leave their set path are called Drifting Stars and are watched with fascination and some frustration.",
                        "At the end of each moon phase the bats hold a Lunar Paean, one part party and two parts ritual, where fortunes are read, children born under the current phase are given star charts, and families look up to name stars for the dead or find their ancestors. Their scholars name every star after its grandeur, which is how one of them came to be called Aurax, the Night Eater and another Blinky. Their magic draws on the cosmos and is sung, sometimes painfully loud. Their homes hang from trees in sweeping shapes like the phases of the moon. The bats of Goodhill sleep in houses on tall poles above the town, and {Zoraline, Cosmos Caller} is the bat who left a great church to study the sky.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Each star is given a name befitting its grandeur. This one is called Aurax, the Night-Eater. That one is called Blinky.",
                        "Starseer Mentor",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-mousefolk", "Mousefolk",
                    listOf(
                        "The mousefolk are red and white, plucky, eager and good hearted, sometimes to the point of naivety. Heroism is their greatest virtue and family pride drives them, not only personal honour but the honour of their whole line. They leap into danger without much planning and somehow find a way through. Mice grow up in big multi generational families with more aunts, uncles and cousins than anyone can count, and community decisions are made by a gathering of their elders. Their homes are cosy many levelled nests of woven grass and leaves with round windows looking out every way.",
                        "Their trials make them tough, from the climb up the Cliff of Heroes to the hunt into the Root Maze for a Calamity's lair. Their weaving makes them fast and strong, and the best of them move so quickly they blur, leaving stuttering afterimages so it seems a whole crowd of mice is attacking. That trick is called blurring, and Mabel is a master of it. Fireflies are the mousefolk's companion insects, and Mabel's children keep one in their bedroom for a night light. In the set the mice are the valiant aggro deck, built around cards like {Mabel, Heir to Cragflame}, {Heartfire Hero} and {Manifold Mouse}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "We sought adventure and found calamity. Forward, so we may meet both with courage!",
                        "Brambleguard Captain",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-rabbitfolk", "Rabbitfolk",
                    listOf(
                        "The rabbitfolk are green and white, the farmers and cooks who put most of Valley's food on the table. Their families, called fluffles, are huge, with the eldest rabbit the arbiter of the household, and many rabbits live their whole lives in one village. They hate confrontation and would rather let trouble blow over. They are so polite that they compete to arrive early, and some have turned up first thing in the morning for a party that starts that night. A gift from a rabbit is measured in volume, to show the family is doing well even when it isn't. Rabbit chefs are thought unrivalled, and many of Valley's inns are run by rabbits.",
                        "Peaceful as they are, rabbits defend their homes. The Pawpatch Party is a band of trained warriors who patrol Valley against the Calamity Beasts in antler helmets with great metal coated weapons, famous for their perseverance and, in no small part, for their excellent provisions. Rabbit magic grows things, sometimes to enormous sizes. Their houses are burrows with windmills on top that double as silos. The set's green and white deck is rabbits going wide with tokens, through cards like {Warren Warleader}, {Finneas, Ace Archer} and {Valley Questcaller}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Many paws grew this feast, and many paws should share it!",
                        "Harvestrite Host",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-frogfolk", "Frogfolk",
                    listOf(
                        "The frogfolk are green and blue augurs who read the migrations of the Calamity Beasts and the turning of the seasons. Because they keep disaster away they are deeply respected, and because they spend their lives foreseeing it they are pessimists, prone to doom and drama. Even a frog who is no augur tends to feel haunted by the bad future everyone keeps predicting. Frogs do not plant fields. They shape the land so that what they want grows by itself, carving terraces into a hill and flooding them for rice or cranberries, or roofing a patch of tender plants against the sun.",
                        "A basic augur sees the rough shape of what is coming. An adept sees exact movements seconds or minutes ahead, and a great one can tell a village to move, a river to be dammed or adventurers to go and draw a beast away. Frog magic is water magic, and many frogs can open small portals that let their blows land from far off. They live in clusters of round houses at the water's edge and play a game called bubbleball. Fountainport is their city and its king was once the most famous augur in Valley. In the set, frogs are the green and blue deck that bounces and replays creatures, with {Clement, the Worrywort} as its legend.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Apprentice augurs begin their studies by predicting where the rain will fall. Even those who don't succeed derive great joy from the process.",
                        "Sunshower Druid",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Bloomburrow, Parts 2 and 3, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-rivers-and-shadows", "Rivers and Shadows",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-ratfolk", "Ratfolk",
                    listOf(
                        "The ratfolk are blue and black, the secret keepers of Valley. The Long River carries scraps of everything it passes down to the marshes where they live, and the rats fish those relics out, record what they are, where they came from and what they were for, and store them in the Knowledge Ossuaries, cavern libraries that spiral down beneath the marshes. They call the sum of their knowledge the World Whorl. They were once open with it, until an interloper used what they knew in a failed attempt to control the Calamity Beasts and nearly destroyed their home. Since then the rats have been isolated, secretive and suspicious.",
                        "Rats are terse loners who bond more with their insect companions than with other folk, and those bonds can grow psychic. Some reach beyond insects. Grotgyre is a snail with hypnotic power strong enough to bend the minds of rats who come close. Rat magic is hypnosis channelled through snail shells and a little telekinesis, made for sending trespassers harmlessly away. Their homes are rows of plain capsule rooms anyone can sleep in, though their plazas are full of art. They get on well with squirrels, who also value reuse, and badly with lizards, who burn their own art. In the set the rats are the threshold deck, and {Wick, the Whorled Mind} and {Vren, the Relentless} lead them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Many rats have found their minds while lost in the mud-slicked gyre of a snail shell.",
                        "Thought Shucker",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-lizardfolk", "Lizardfolk",
                    listOf(
                        "The lizardfolk are black and red, and the only animalfolk not native to Valley. Many seasons ago they settled its few shaley hills and rocky ravines, which look most like the lands they came from. They are industrious, cantankerous fire worshippers who live at their own pace. They lack the polished hospitality of other folk and grumble as they help, but the work gets done. They hold grudges for a long time, and once they forgive, they never bring it up again. Owing a favour makes a lizard uneasy until it is paid back. Too much sun leaves them twitchy and frantic for somewhere to spend the energy.",
                        "Lizard art is meant to be destroyed. A community makes something to inspire awe, and when it grows bored of the piece it breaks or burns it and makes the next one from its pieces, believing the spirit of the work lives on. Every lizard settlement has a central fire, and lizards keep the flames of other villages lit through long winters and guide wildfires away from homes. They are master smiths and fiery shamans and quiet assassins, and some can mix venom into their flames. In the set they are the deck that punishes opponents for losing life, with {Gev, Scaled Scorch} at their head.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "We don't 'play' with fire. It is strictly business.",
                        "Cindering Cutthroat",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-raccoonfolk", "Raccoonfolk",
                    listOf(
                        "The raccoonfolk are red and green wanderers and hoarders, as at home on the road as in any village. To them no duty matters more than discovering things, which other folk take for flakiness. They will join a journey gladly and leave it just as suddenly, then greet an old friend years later as if they had parted yesterday. Their attention spans are short. Ownership means little to them and they are never surprised to find a guest in the house, though they expect their trinkets treated with respect.",
                        "Those trinkets are everything. A raccoon carries mementos from every trip, broken teapots and bent swords and pebbles, each holding a memory, in vine knapsacks heavy enough to swing as a club. Their histories are passed down by word of mouth and come out colourful and full of holes. An invitation to a raccoon's house usually means a going away party that starts late, runs long, and ends with a story everyone builds a piece of. Their druids turn junk into missiles and walls and can wake the land itself, and their homes are sturdy dens in tree roots that are really storage. In the set the raccoons are the expend deck, which rewards spending a lot of mana in a turn, with {Muerra, Trash Tactician} as their legend.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "For the last time it's porcelain, not stoneware, you ignorant fool!",
                        "Teapot Slinger",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-otterfolk", "Otterfolk",
                    listOf(
                        "The otterfolk are blue and red, playful thrill seekers who treat everything as a game. Some of them chase storms for sport, following the Calamity Beasts as close as they dare to pluck a hair from a storm bearer or catch an ember from a burning wolverine. They bottle the beasts' magic in shells, pearls and glass, which they call clamming, and they prize lightning above all. Otters forgive big wrongs once amends are made and keep small grudges forever. Many go their whole lives without setting foot on dry land, living on houseboats lashed together into floating villages called Rafts.",
                        "Their great pastime is otterball, an endless game whose rules nobody outside the otters has ever worked out. All anyone knows is that it is mostly played in water and that the Calamity Beasts are involved somehow. Otters are Valley's best divers and fishers, its river guides and ferry folk, and its acrobats at every festival. They get along best with rabbits and worst with frogs, who find them irreverent while the otters find the frogs boring. In the set the otters are the spellslinger deck, with {Kitsa, Otterball Elite}, {Alania, Divergent Storm} and {Stormcatch Mentor}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Why would I chase the storm? Does a cloud chase the rain? Does the sun chase the light?",
                        "Alania, Divergent Storm",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-squirrelfolk", "Squirrelfolk",
                    listOf(
                        "The squirrelfolk are black and green, the haughty, morbid stewards of Valley's earth. They are solitary and work with others only when they must. They bury the remains of Calamity Beasts, raise the creatures that break down the dead, and restore tired soil. They believe the Calamities trap energy that should flow through nature, and that burying the bones returns it. Death, to a squirrel, is simply part of the cycle, and they look down on folk who fear it. A squirrel expression for dying is sleeping beneath the sumac.",
                        "They are also Valley's pantry. Squirrels keep great caches of pickled, dried and salted food against the sudden seasons the beasts bring, sometimes stored next to Calamity bones in hope of soaking up their power, and much of Valley depends on those stores. A few practise true necromancy, raising and commanding bones, a secret taught to very few. Raising other animalfolk is taboo, but insects and Calamity Beasts are fair game. Children of other folk dare each other to knock on a squirrel's door and run. Their homes are lonely houses on stilts or rotting trees, often built around old Calamity bones. In the set squirrels are the forage deck, with {Camellia, the Seedmiser} as their legend.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "We all share one life and one death.",
                        "Rotcaller saying, Valley Rotcaller",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-everyone-else", "Everyone Else",
                    listOf(
                        "The ten peoples of the draft decks are only the start. Valley also holds badgerfolk like Hugs, the big gruff warrior of the story, and weaselfolk, among them the stoat Brightblades who angle their glass daggers to throw sunlight into enemy eyes, and the weasel mercenary Cruelclaw. There are molefolk like the scholar Tucker, skunkfolk whose stink spells can lay out a fighter, possums, hedgehogs, voles, chipmunks, gerbils, minks, beavers, turtles and foxfolk. The set introduced Skunk and Weasel as creature types for the first time. Folk of different kinds marry one another, and nobody thinks twice.",
                        "Every people has an insect it pairs with and a construct it rides. Mice keep fireflies for light, bats keep luna moths, rats bond with snails, and ladybugs are raised as livestock. Mussels serve as alarm bells along the river. Constructs of wood and even of cabbages carry folk along the roads, and a cabbage carriage on spidery legs brings the heroes home at the end of the story. Fish are both food and currency, which is why so many cards in the set give an opponent a little blue Fish as a present. The animals of Valley are mostly drawn from the eastern United States, while the lizards come from all over the world, as fits a people who arrived from somewhere else.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Bloomburrow, Parts 2 and 3, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Bloomburrow plane"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-calamity-comes", "Calamity Comes",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-pondside-burns", "Pondside Burns",
                    listOf(
                        "The story opens on a frog who did not fit. Helga lived in Pondside, a small village by a pond, in the house her late grandparents had left her. She could not keep her mind on chores. She ruined a batch of witch hazel medicine by forgetting the fire, and her neighbours scolded her for staring into the water looking for visions instead of working. She had once been an apprentice to King Glarb in Fountainport and that had ended badly too. What she had was her journal, full of patterns and sketches and, now and then, pictures her hand drew by itself.",
                        "That morning she had drawn something terrible, a creature like the Sun Hawk from the king's illustrated books, but with four wings webbed like a bat's, a crest swept back like a fin and a forked tongue, against a background of storm. She went looking for the village augur to show him, knowing he would probably dismiss it. Before she found him the warning bell started ringing.",
                        "Maha, the Night Owl, was circling overhead in perfect silence, night pouring out behind its wings. It swooped and a house burst apart. Sparks from a cook fire caught the wreckage and smoke and flames spread through the village. Helga ran through patches of day and dark, lost among the cabbages, until she tumbled down a bank into Pond Creek. When she rolled over the sky above was black and full of strange stars. She could have hidden in the pond and waited. Instead she followed the creek toward the next village to bring help, bruised and heartsick, limping toward the distant sunshine.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-mabels-birthday", "Mabel's Birthday",
                    listOf(
                        "In Goodhill a few hours up the creek, it was Mabel's birthday in late spring. Mabel was a mouse, a baker, and the mother of three, Rosalyn the solemn eldest and the squabbling Foggy and Pip, who were trying to hang a birthday banner by standing on each other's shoulders. Her husband Clem, a plump grey mouse and an even better baker, was out trading for elderberry jam. The whole town was coming that evening for strawberry cake, tarts, muffins, crumble and carrot cake.",
                        "Oliver, the rabbitfolk mayor, came round with a request Mabel had been dreading. Silver the bard planned to tell the tale of the Order of the Holly Leaf at the party, and Oliver hoped she might bring down the old relic from her attic to show everyone. Mabel's family had guarded it for generations, and her mother Iris had never once brought it out for the neighbours to gawk at. Mabel fenced him off as politely as she could. Then the children's tower toppled, Mabel caught two of them in midair, and Pip declared his mother a hero.",
                        "A weaselfolk named Jenefer came running with news from the lookout. There was a stranger in Pond Creek, and she did not look well. Mabel took her thistle hilted rapier from the wall and ran. She found a young frog collapsed in the mud, who opened her eyes long enough to say there had been an attack by a Calamity Beast, and fainted. Mabel had her carried to her own home, looked to the sky for any sign of the beast, and understood that her party would have to wait.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-the-storm-mage", "A Storm Mage Lost",
                    listOf(
                        "Far away on Ravnica, one thought kept surfacing in Ral Zarek's head. [Jace Beleren](who/jace) was alive. For nearly two years Ral had believed him dead in the Phyrexian invasion, and then on [Thunder Junction](thunder-junction) he had found out that Jace had been disguised as the nightmare Ashiok in order to steal some strange creature from a vault. Ral wanted to find him. He also told his husband Tomik, more than once, that he meant to kill him. Tomik told him he wouldn't, because Jace was his friend, and that he would never learn why if he did.",
                        "Ral had tried planeswalking to Jace and ended up on a beach on Ixalan. So he went to the one person who might track him, the Wanderer, in her palace garden on Kamigawa. She could not help. Her spark was gone, and she was at peace without it, one of the many [who lost theirs](sparks-lost) after the invasion. She told him planeswalkers leave trails through the [Blind Eternities](the-blind-eternities), and that he should go back to the last place he had seen Jace and open his soul.",
                        "So Ral went to the ruins of Tarnation where the vault had been. It was empty. He reached out with his storm magic and felt something faint, a hint of green, smudged like a word half rubbed out. He followed it into the Blind Eternities and came out in a field of dandelions taller than any he had ever seen. He raised a hand to his face and found whiskers, fur and a tail. Ral Zarek was an otter, and he was furious about it.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-an-expected-party", "An Expected Party",
                    listOf(
                        "Helga woke in Mabel's house and told her story to Mabel, Clem and the mayor, the Night Owl, the ruined village and the dream of a scarred weasel in a red hood at every attack, and the drawing of the strange hawk. Oliver decided she had an artist's overactive imagination and that all was well. The Spring Elk had come on time, he told the crowd outside. The Sun Hawk was not due yet. The wild ones, the Blight Snake and the Drought Cat, had not troubled Valley since the Battle of Kell's Ridge. Mabel thought otherwise. She believed Helga's visions were real.",
                        "She climbed to the attic and uncovered the heirloom. It was a sword made from a massive tooth, sigil etched and wickedly curved, its pommel carved as a dancing flame. The story said it was Cragflame, made by Lily of Valley from the fang of the Wildfire Wolf. Her mother had doubted that, but said the sword had power either way, and that it did not take kindly to thieves. When Mabel lifted it, warmth ran through her from ear to tail and the sigils glowed red for the first time.",
                        "At the party, after Oliver's speech praising her, Mabel told Goodhill that Helga had been brave, that Pondside needed help, and that if a Calamity Beast was loose nobody was safe for long. She raised the sword and fire rippled down the blade. Then she asked who would come. Finneas the archer volunteered first, over his sisters' protests. The huge badger Hugs rose and nodded, so his partner Gev the lizard came too, to keep him out of trouble. The bat Zoraline came because the stars unsettled her. Last came Helga, because it was her home. They left at first light.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Something has stirred the Calamity Beasts, and they are wreaking havoc. Anyone who wishes to help, come forth! Whether it's with your swords in the fight or your shovels in the field, join me!",
                        "Mabel, heir to Cragflame, Galewind Moose",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Bloomburrow, Episode 1: Calamity Comes to Valley, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Bloomburrow, Episode 2: An Expected Party, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Mabel"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-river-and-storm", "River and Storm",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-bones-at-pondside", "Bones at Pondside",
                    listOf(
                        "The party walked out of Goodhill past fields of beets and turnips into wilder meadows, Zoraline asleep upside down on Hugs's back, as she would be for most of the journey. On the way Gev told the tale of the Striped Rapscallions, himself, Hugs and a skunk named Kiki, who once fought a bone monster with six heads in the Calamity Graveyard. Kiki, he said, had since fallen in love with a raccoon and settled down to raise ladybugs. Mabel quietly told Helga that Gev told tales, and that whatever really happened beyond the Outer Woods was too painful for him and Hugs to speak of.",
                        "Pondside was charred wicker, broken clay and a door banging in the wind. Tracks showed the survivors had gone south toward Haymeadow. Then Gev spotted two squirrelfolk picking through the rubble, necromancers in bone decked black, complaining that their boss Cruelclaw had them combing ruins and that the owl had made a mess of this place just like the last one. When one of them threw a child's painting into the pond, Helga shouted at them. They raised dozens of skeletons, rats and otters and birds wrapped in purple light.",
                        "Mabel blurred through them with an orange flash of Cragflame. Finneas loosed arrows and a burr that burst into binding vines, Gev spun his fire maces, Hugs tore skeletons apart, and Zoraline drained a necromancer's magic and released it in a keening prayer song that dropped the undead where they stood. Helga built a stair of water droplets so Mabel could reach the flying skeletons, then lost her concentration halfway up. Mabel caught herself anyway. The necromancers fled toward the Long River, and the party chose to follow them to Three Tree City rather than go to Haymeadow. Helga took her grandmother's copper orb, her grandfather's wand and his wide hat from her ruined house.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-the-flood-gar", "The Flood Gar",
                    listOf(
                        "A family of otterfolk on their way to a wedding gave them a ride downriver in painted fish shaped boats, and Gev lost his breakfast over the side. At the Docklands of Three Tree City, Mabel and Finneas asked after the necromancers and Cruelclaw with no luck, until an old frog mentioned another stranger asking questions all day. It was a black furred otter in goggles and a copper bracer who kept slapping people with his tail as if he forgot it was his. He was describing a foxfolk with odd markings to a crowd that laughed at his drawing.",
                        "Helga recognised him. She had drawn that fox's face in her journal. She told the otter, who called himself Ral Zarek and was looking for [Jace](who/jace) Beleren, that she had seen the fox in her dreams with a lizard of green and black scales, a small creature hard to make out, and a dark cloud following them. Ral believed her. In Helga's mind he was the first, other than Mabel, who ever had. He announced he was not letting her out of his sight.",
                        "Then the mussel alarms clattered. The river rose past the high water mark and over the quay, boats tore from their moorings, and something huge moved beneath the surface, silver scaled and marked with glowing runes. It was the Flood Gar, Eluge itself. Whips of water swept folk into the river. Ral raised his crackling bracer and Mabel stopped him, because lightning would hit everyone in the water. A wave took them all. Hugs kept them afloat as the current carried them past the city and down a tributary to the southwest, until he dragged them onto the mud of a marshy cave full of old bones, where hooded ratfolk with curved daggers surrounded them and told them to leave or die.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Zoraline turned her attention to the shore. Helga and Ral's conversation quickly faded from her mind as she watched the gigantic wave approach.",
                        "Calamitous Tide",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-the-lorekeepers", "The Lorekeepers",
                    listOf(
                        "An elder rat with one black eye and one red, leaning on a cane with a snail shell handle, called off the guards. His name was Coffey, and the river, he said, had brought these strangers, so they would have hospitality. He led them down through limestone tunnels dug ages ago by enormous insects to a cosy study, where a molefolk scholar named Tucker served chamomile tea and seedcake. The rats were lorekeepers who gathered every scrap of history that washed up on their shores. Cruelclaw's band had passed through days before, Coffey said, the Night Owl wreaking havoc in its wake, and the rats' scouts had seen what he carried. It was a Calamity Beast's egg.",
                        "Mabel said at once that they would find whoever had hired him and return the egg. Finneas protested that they were a farmer, a cleric and two mercenaries, not heroes. Ral said he would keep them all alive while Helga was his only link to Jace, and muttered something about setting up a relay tower. Mabel answered that knowing about the problem made it theirs.",
                        "To find Cruelclaw they needed Helga's augury, and for the first time someone asked her to use it on purpose. She stared into a basin of water and nothing came, until Zoraline told her the light was already inside her, she need only uncover it. Helga stopped forcing it and began to doodle a snail shell, and her hand drew on by itself. When she looked down, she had sketched a three tiered fountain rising from a pond. The mercenaries had gone to Fountainport.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-the-storm-hawk", "The Storm Hawk",
                    listOf(
                        "Tucker warned them that the fastest way, across a dandelion field, might not be safe. A great storm some days before had brought a creature with it that was not a Calamity Beast but very like one, and the lorekeepers had no record of anything similar. Ral muttered that it might be from another plane. They went anyway. On the walk Finneas asked Ral about home, and Ral, unusually, admitted he missed his husband. Mabel said she missed hers too.",
                        "Then a shadow passed over and the clouds began to swirl. The creature from Helga's drawing hung in the air, four wings webbed at the tips, a lizard's crest, purple lightning crawling over it. Ral said it was a dragon, changed into an animal like everyone else who came here, and as dangerous as any Calamity. The party crept on under the dandelions until Zoraline, still asleep on Hugs, sneezed herself awake and asked loudly why everyone was being so quiet.",
                        "The beast dove. Ral asked for a lightning rod, and Finneas shot an arrow wound with copper wire into its mouth. Zoraline's glissando scrambled its senses. Ral called down a barrage of lightning that struck the arrow and the beast together, bright as a second sun, and the dragon hawk writhed but did not fall. It flew off into the clouds. The party sheltered under a tilted boulder while Helga held off the rain with a shield of water, a cantrip she managed perfectly, and Ral walked out of the storm dry, waving the clouds away.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Give me a lightning rod, something I can use as a channel! Ral called above the mayhem. Nodding, Finneas drew a copperleaf arrow from his emptying quiver and shot.",
                        "Conduct Electricity",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Bloomburrow, Episode 3: The Lost and the Found, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Bloomburrow, Episode 4: Soothsaying and Stormcalling, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Helga"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-nightfall", "Nightfall",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-his-moistness", "His Moistness",
                    listOf(
                        "Fountainport rose over Valley's largest pond, its spire of water visible long before they reached the floating markets of the Tadpool. Helga suggested, shaking, that she might get them an audience with King Glarb, since she had been his apprentice. They rode the glass elevators up the tiers, past domed frog houses on their own bowls and boats with silver embroidered sails, to the top, where walls were made of falling water and the throne room looked out over everything.",
                        "While they waited, Mabel asked what exactly Helga had done to be sent away. Helga confessed. They had been practising waterspout cantrips, knocking down gnats, and she had hit the king's staff by mistake. It fell, and she opened a portal under it and another by her hand to catch it, just as the king sent a water whip after it. The whip went through her portal and struck the king, then the staff fell on his head. He spent days recovering, and the staff broke. Mabel told her the past informs who we are but need not define us.",
                        "The aide, who called the king His Moistness, took them in. Glarb sat on a throne of floral tracery and greeted Helga with open disdain. When Mabel told him a weasel named Cruelclaw had stolen a Calamity egg and been tracked to his city, Helga saw the king's eyes flick to a leaf screen beside the throne, where something powerful pulsed like a heartbeat. He asked who else knew. Then he told them that sometimes sacrifices must be made for the greater good.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-the-egg", "The Egg",
                    listOf(
                        "Helga pulled the screen down with a tendril of water. Behind it, in an ornate nest, sat a huge egg with a velvety blue shell flecked with specks like stars. Glarb did not deny it. He had paid Cruelclaw very well to bring it, and the weasel stepped out from behind the throne, scarred over one eye, and said the Direshade Company fulfilled its contracts. Glarb explained his dream. With the right weaving he would control the beast inside, and his own champion would drive the other Calamities away forever. Helga told him he had flooded Three Tree City and burned her village, and asked if he wanted to become like the mad weavers of old.",
                        "Glarb bound them all in ropes of water, set a dozen mercenaries on them, and told Helga she was a failure as a weaver. It was the thing she had always told herself, and she sank to her knees. Mabel told her she was only a failure if she gave up. So Helga used the smallest spell she knew. She slid a water step under Cruelclaw's feet and sent him skidding into the king, and Glarb's binding broke. The art of {Mabel's Mettle} shows Helga and Glarb in that throne room.",
                        "Hugs and Gev took on two raccoons, one of them hurling a whirlwind of teapots. Zoraline blew a songbird off the edge. Finneas planted burrs in a skunk's backside and Ral zapped a leaping lizard. Mabel faced Cruelclaw, whose rapier left green poison on her shield. Glarb opened a portal in her path and the weasel's blade came down toward her neck, and she thought of Clem and the children, and Cragflame burst into flame. She drove him to the edge and knocked his sword away. He told her to finish it. She told him to leave with his life and make better choices.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "You stole a child from its nest, Mabel accused. The only calamity I see here is you.",
                        "Mabel's Mettle",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-the-night-owl", "The Night Owl",
                    listOf(
                        "Glarb had frozen Helga in a block of ice. Mabel swung at him and a great blaze leapt from the sword, shaped for a heartbeat like a wolf in mid leap, and the king flinched. She thrust the burning blade into the ice and it melted like sugar in hot water. Glarb rose on a pillar of water, casting hooked lines to snare them, and called them a pack of farmers and a failure who could never understand his vision. Above him the sky went dark.",
                        "Lightning split the clouds and the Storm Hawk dove at the king like a lance. Glarb vanished through a portal. Ral could not strike back, since lightning on the water spire might kill everyone in the city touching water, so he worked to calm the storm. Mabel had Helga raise water stairs up one of the arches to the stone lily at the top of the fountain, climbed to it, and held up her flaming sword as a challenge. The beast came for her with its talons out.",
                        "Out of nowhere the Night Owl struck it, trailing a curtain of stars. The two great birds fought across the sky, the owl always a step ahead, until Maha tore a feather from the hawk's tail and the hawk fled, taking the storm with it. Then the owl landed on the rim of the basin and looked at Mabel. She climbed down, picked up the egg, larger than she was, and carried it to the beast that could have swallowed her whole. She told it she would have been beside herself with worry too. Maha took the egg gently, its feathers settling, and flew away into a night that now felt peaceful. The art of {Dawn's Truce} shows that moment.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "When Glarb ordered the egg stolen, the Great Maha communicated its anger in a universal language.",
                        "Fell",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-home-to-goodhill", "Home to Goodhill",
                    listOf(
                        "Glarb fled not only the throne room but Fountainport, and nobody knew where he had gone. Cruelclaw escaped too. Some of his Direshades surrendered and the rest followed him. City officials promised investigations and justice. Mabel decided her part of the story was finished, and a cabbage carriage on spidery legs carried them all home, which Ral found deeply unsettling. Goodhill met them with a parade, banging pots and pans. Over Mabel's door hung a new banner for her return, with a hasty postscript for everyone else, and Oliver climbed onto a box to make another speech.",
                        "Helga decided to stay in Goodhill as its hedgewitch, doing weather auguries and cantrips, and Mabel told her to get used to being called a hero on her birthday. Ral announced he was leaving, since Jace was clearly not on this world. Then Helga's eyes lit like a mirror catching the sun and a voice not quite her own spoke a prophecy about kings in the dark and a mage in blue. A moment later she had no memory of it and asked what kind of muffins there were. The story leaves it unexplained.",
                        "Before Ral went, Mabel ran up to her attic and gave him a pair of ironsap buttons embossed with holly leaves, one for him and one for his husband, and told him he would always be welcome. He said he could not say he had loved having a tail. Then he vanished in a burst of lightning, and Mabel told her children nothing more than they needed to know. The moon came up over Goodhill with a shooting star across the blackberry sky. [Jace](who/jace) was still out there with his lizard and his small strange companion, and the dragon that should not have been in Valley had escaped into the sky.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The kings in the dark will return. The mage in blue will bring about the end.",
                        "Helga, Bloomburrow Episode 5: Nightfall in Fountainport",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Bloomburrow, Episode 5: Nightfall in Fountainport, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Glarb"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Maha"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-mabels-party", "Mabel's Party",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-mabel", "Mabel",
                    listOf(
                        "{Mabel, Heir to Cragflame} is the heart of the set and the face on its Play Booster. She is a baker, a hero, and most proudly a mother, living in Goodhill with Clem and their three children. As a girl she was a wild one, always in mischief and never home by dark. She climbed the Cliff of Heroes with a band of young mice, and as a grown mouse she stood with the town's defenders when bandits came, an episode she mostly remembers for landing in a skunk's stink spell and scrubbing her fur for ages. Then family was adventure enough. She carries a rapier of ironsap with a thistle hilt and a small shield painted with a five petalled mallow flower, wears an orange dress with a red holly button, and is a master of blurring.",
                        "She is descended, so the family story goes, from Lily Emberseed, and the sword she inherited from her mother Iris is Cragflame. She was never sure she could live up to that. What she has instead is a managing temperament, a practical mind and enormous kindness. She was the first in Goodhill to believe Helga. She spared Cruelclaw because it was not the story she wanted to tell her children, and she returned the egg because she would have wanted her own child returned. On the card her other Mice get bigger, and she brings Cragflame into play as a legendary Equipment token that grants vigilance, trample and haste. {Valley Rally} shows her too.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-helga", "Helga",
                    listOf(
                        "{Helga, Skittish Seer} is a small grey green frog with amber eyes and a permanent nervous smile, born in a pond near Three Tree City that dried up one summer. She followed her grandparents to Pondside, where everyone expected her to become an augur, and she spent her tadpole years staring at ripples instead. She was sent to Glarb's school in Fountainport and did even worse, shaken by his harshness, until the accident with his staff ended it. She is a persistent pessimist, an artist whose journal holds abstract patterns, sketches of what she sees, and visions she never meant to draw, and she struggles to focus on anything that does not interest her.",
                        "She knows only a few cantrips, a stair of water droplets, a rain shield, a slippery step, and over the journey she learns that small magic can be big enough. Her visions find Jace's trail for Ral, her augury finds Fountainport, and her water step under Cruelclaw breaks the king's spell. She chose to stay in Goodhill afterward, among friends who could help her grow, and the story leaves her last prophecy unexplained. Her card is green, white and blue, a frog druid who taps for a lot of mana toward big creatures. She also appears in the art of {Portent of Calamity}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-gev-and-hugs", "Gev and Hugs",
                    listOf(
                        "{Hugs, Grisly Guardian} is a scarred old badger with an eyepatch and a white striped head, three times Mabel's height. He grew up alone in the Root Maze with no friends but the insects he eventually ate, and he could not speak and had no name when a lizard named Gev stumbled on him. They became inseparable, mercenaries and partners. On an early job Hugs held off a Calamity Beast, a moose, while Gev led a town to safety, and when the townsfolk came back he stood alone, wounded but upright. The town's children threw their arms around him. He knelt and hugged them back, and Gev knew exactly what to call him. In the story he carries the sleeping Zoraline the whole way and keeps every one of them afloat in the flood.",
                        "{Gev, Scaled Scorch} has handsome red circles around his eyes, a tail that glows orange when he heats up and a pair of fire maces he spins like a dancer. He wanted to be an artist as a child and would say he is one now, working in blades and attitude, and he has softened over the years under Hugs's influence. Something terrible happened to the two of them and their skunk friend Kiki beyond the Outer Woods, and Gev copes by telling tall tales, though when they got home he told the story of the quest to Goodhill with surprising accuracy. Both of them joined Mabel because Hugs did.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Most of his scars tell tales. A few tell jokes.",
                        "Hugs, Grisly Guardian",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-finneas-and-zoraline", "Finneas and Zoraline",
                    listOf(
                        "{Finneas, Ace Archer} is a sable furred rabbit farmer in a wide straw hat from a long line of Goodhill farmers, and the town's archery champion so often that he was banned from the spring and autumn contests and now judges them. He took up archery to pass the time, until his mother saw him nearly hit a target and signed him up for every lesson she could find. He dreams of joining the Pawpatch Party. He was the first to volunteer for Mabel's quest and the first to say aloud that they were only ordinary folk. His trick arrows, one bursting into vines, one wound with copper wire, saved them twice.",
                        "{Zoraline, Cosmos Caller} is a dark furred bat with a dreamy voice and flowing pale green robes. She comes from one of the oldest bat lineages in Valley, traced back to the time of Lily, and is secretly second in line to be head cleric of the largest church in Three Tree City. The pompous ceremonies did not suit her and she left with only a note, stopping in Goodhill for a night's rest that became seven. She sleeps through nearly every scene, but when awake she drains enemy magic and sings it back as a keening prayer, and she gave Helga the advice that made her augury work.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-ral-in-valley", "Ral in Valley",
                    listOf(
                        "Ral Zarek is the outsider of the party, the Izzet storm mage from Ravnica who was one of the few planeswalkers to keep his spark after the invasion. On Bloomburrow he became an otter with black fur streaked with white, goggles, a red sash and his copper bracer, and he spent the whole story forgetting he had a tail. He came looking for [Jace](who/jace) and stayed to protect the people who had his only lead, insisting it was purely practical. He rubbed the white cloth tied around his wrist when he admitted to Mabel that he missed his husband, one of the few soft things he let himself say.",
                        "The plane read him as an otterfolk, which confused everyone, since he controlled rain rather than rivers. He brought down the lightning that drove off the dragon hawk, and he was the one who recognised it as a dragon. His card, {Ral, Crackling Wit}, is a blue and red planeswalker that makes otter tokens with prowess and grows whenever a noncreature spell is cast, with an ultimate that gives instants and sorceries storm. The Bundle box shows him as an otter, and the card also has a borderless version.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-the-villains", "Glarb and Cruelclaw",
                    listOf(
                        "{Glarb, Calamity's Augur} was not always a schemer. He once gently ruled Fountainport and ran a great school of augury, famous both for his knowledge and for his care for the cycles that gave Valley life. Then for many seasons he dreamed of Valley destroyed by Calamity Beasts, and the visions wore at his mind and his temper. Unwilling to let it happen, he hired Cruelclaw to steal Maha's egg so he could raise the beast himself as Valley's protector, sure that no cost could be worse than what he had seen. The cost was a burned village and a drowned city, and when he lost he fled Fountainport. He uses water and ice in battle and walks through portals. {Diresight} shows him.",
                        "{The Infamous Cruelclaw} is a weaselfolk mercenary in a hooded red tailcoat with three claw scars over his right eye and a poisoned rapier. He has been a lover sometimes and a scoundrel most of the time. He began by stealing raccoon heirlooms from empty dens and moved on to nastier clients, with a reputation for never turning down a job and never losing a fight, though he has never fought a fair one. He leads the Direshade Company, a dozen or more squirrel necromancers, birds, lizards, rabbits, raccoons and skunks. Mabel beat him and let him go, and he escaped. {Cruelclaw's Heist} is the story spotlight card for the theft that started everything.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Calamity Beasts are running through Valley, leaving death and destruction in their wake. I have a plan to stop them.",
                        "Glarb, ruler of Fountainport, Quaketusk Boar",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Bloomburrow, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Bloomburrow, Episodes 1 to 5, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Mabel's party"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-legends-of-valley", "Legends of Valley",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-guardians", "Guardians",
                    listOf(
                        "Beyond the party, the set is full of legends with stories of their own. {Baylen, the Haymaker} is a rabbit, part time farmer and part time warrior, who lived for a while among the lizards of Three Tree City learning their fiery ways before slipping off to rural Haymeadow to farm in peace. They were scouted anyway, made a Pawpatch recruit and invited into the Party itself as the Calamities grew restless. When one came for Haymeadow, Baylen poured raw magic into a scythe and drove it off, then left the Party rather than leave the village undefended. {Kastral, the Windcrested} is a bird squad leader who came home from her Plumecreed seven seasons after her own funeral. A strange, massive lightning storm had blown her far off course with a broken wing, and she survived with the help of folk she had never seen, running from Calamities no one had heard of. She came back faster than any other Windcrested.",
                        "{Muerra, Trash Tactician} leads a band of raccoons who guard Scratchbark, the country leading to the Root Maze, fighting raiders for fun and rescuing naive travellers for a fee in materials. Her most famous trick was using light magic to make a sunspine lynx attack itself. {Clement, the Worrywort} is the safest adventurer in Valley, a nervous frog whose parties set out in perfect weather with little danger and a respectable reward, which rather defeats the point, though when trouble comes he is ready with provisions, directions and destruction. He is said to bake fine seedloaf, if you can get past the slime.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-schemers-and-mystics", "Schemers and Mystics",
                    listOf(
                        "{Wick, the Whorled Mind} grew up in the marsh village of Conch, a prodigy among psychic rats, bonded so closely to his snail Grotgyre that each could anticipate the other's thoughts. Then a sickness took the snails of Conch and Wick with them. Haunted by visions of whorls and eyes, he stopped sleeping and wrote scripture through the nights, until his paranoia became enlightenment. He left Conch with a few followers to preach and now tunnels spiral labyrinths into the earth, where each new convert is bonded to an anointed snail. They worship the snails, eat them, turn what is left into magic, and bond again, around and around forever.",
                        "{Vren, the Relentless} follows the trails of the Calamity Beasts to salvage what they leave behind, and when there is nothing to salvage he makes some. His gang would fill a small rabbit village. When a beast must be driven off rather than fled, he sets up shop and sells arms and armour at a bargain, because the margins on recycled goods are nearly total. {Camellia, the Seedmiser} has a near monopoly on Valley's seeds, hoarding them until the first snow or famine. Bakers and farmers dread her name, but she grew up hungry in the Brambles, and Valley has never wanted for food since her caches filled.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "We call it the Season of Long Night, whose wingspan brings darkness that blots out the sun from the sky, and in its wake, we can see the constellations of the night sky above and every night sky before it.",
                        "Wick, the whorled mind, The Legends of Bloomburrow",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-otters-and-rogues", "Otters and Rogues",
                    listOf(
                        "{Alania, Divergent Storm} is a mysterious otter mage who can copy magic at a glance and travels where others will not, always asking why. She joins only parties that promise something new, and has gathered self appointed apprentices who follow her whether she likes it or not. After she beat a rottenmouth viper alone by copying its own powers, she started asking why not instead. {Kitsa, Otterball Elite} has broken so many tides and bones that her parent claims her blood is water. Her rivals swear her twitching whiskers see through every play, and they have invented whole strategies to block her view, which she finds funny.",
                        "{Bria, Riptide Rogue} is a thief who has plagued Fountainport for seasons. She once planted a banner above the throne room promising free food courtesy of Glarb, and while Glarb and his guards dispersed the hungry crowd, the shops below were emptied, a few shells left as her calling card. She runs on currents of water in midair. {Byrke, Long Ear of the Law} is the rabbit constable who chases her, famous for lecturing every rascal he arrests until they reform rather than hear another, and he has not slept a full night since she arrived. The two of them are the face cards of the Starter Kit. {Flubs, the Fool} is a frog said to be the luckiest creature alive, who has dodged arrows by bending to smell flowers and is the only animalfolk ever to escape Lumra's forests. Since the Omenpaths opened he has stumbled from world to world and picked up a following.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "He knows exactly where he's going and exactly how he isn't getting there.",
                        "Flubs, the Fool",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Bloomburrow, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow Starter Kit"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-mechanics", "Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-ten-animal-pairs", "Ten Animal Pairs",
                    listOf(
                        "Bloomburrow is a typal set. Every two colour pair in draft is one kind of animal with its own plan. White and blue birds fly over everything. Blue and black rats play control and care about threshold, having seven or more cards in the graveyard, which returned for them. Black and red lizards attack and reward every point of life an opponent loses. Red and green raccoons are midrange ramp built around expend. Green and white rabbits go wide with tokens. White and black bats drain life and gain it. Blue and red otters cast noncreature spells and grow with prowess. Black and green squirrels forage through Food and the graveyard. Red and white mice are valiant aggro. Green and blue frogs bounce their own creatures to reuse what they do when they enter.",
                        "Each colour sits in four of those ten pairs, and the set uses that shape. Five rare Valley callers, among them {Valley Questcaller}, each reward the four animals of their colour, and only one of those animals is on the card while the other three appear in the background of the art. The five uncommon villages tap for coloured mana only to cast creatures and do something extra when one of their four animals arrives. Ten uncommon creatures give each animal a payoff in a single colour, such as {Flowerfoot Swordmaster} for mice and {Knightfisher} for birds. And the legendary land {Three Tree City} names any creature type and taps for mana equal to how many of them you control.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-offspring-and-gift", "Offspring and Gift",
                    listOf(
                        "Offspring is a new keyword for creatures and an optional extra cost. Pay it as you cast the creature and when it enters you also get a token copy of it, identical except that it is a 1/1. The small copy keeps every ability, so an offspring creature with a strong trigger simply does its job twice. Each offspring card has its own printed token with fresh art of the little one. {Manifold Mouse}, {Warren Warleader}, {Thundertrap Trainer} and {Tender Wildguide} all carry it.",
                        "Gift is the set's spirit of generosity turned into a rule, and is also an optional extra cost. As you cast a gift card you may promise a present to one opponent. Depending on the card the gift is a card, a tapped 1/1 blue Fish, a Food or something else, and the card does more, or something different, if you promised. For a permanent the opponent receives it when the permanent enters. For an instant or sorcery they receive it first, as the spell begins to resolve. If the spell is countered nobody gets anything. {Kitnap} steals a creature, and only if you let an opponent draw a card does it arrive without three stun counters. {Parting Gust} hands over a Fish and exiles the target for good. {Starfall Invocation} destroys every creature and, if you gifted a card, returns one of yours.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-forage-expend-valiant", "Forage, Expend, Valiant",
                    listOf(
                        "Forage is a new keyword action and usually a cost. To forage you either exile three cards from your graveyard or sacrifice a Food, which ties the squirrels' graveyard plan to the Food tokens that run through the set. A card that cares when you forage only counts the actual instruction, not any old sacrifice. {Osteomancer Adept} lets you cast creatures from your graveyard by foraging as you do, and {Camellia, the Seedmiser} forages to grow every other squirrel.",
                        "Expend counts the mana you have spent casting spells this turn, including extra costs like offspring. Expend four triggers as you spend your fourth mana, and only once a turn. It is the raccoons' mechanic, and {Junkblade Bruiser} and {Muerra, Trash Tactician} show its thresholds of four and eight. Valiant is a new ability word, a cousin of heroic, for abilities that trigger when a creature becomes the target of a spell or ability you control for the first time each turn. It works off abilities as well as spells and fires on any player's turn, which made it ideal for the mice, as on {Seedglaive Mentor}. The set also brought back flashback, landfall and hybrid mana, put changeling on two artifact creatures, and gave Ral's ultimate a cameo of storm.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-seasons-and-talents", "Seasons and Talents",
                    listOf(
                        "The five mythic Seasons use a new symbol, the pawprint. Each Season lists three modes worth one, two and three pawprints, and you choose up to five pawprints worth, repeating any mode you like. {Season of the Burrow} can make five rabbits, or exile two permanents and make a rabbit, or return a cheap permanent with an indestructible counter and exile something besides. {Season of Weaving}, {Season of Loss}, {Season of the Bold} and {Season of Gathering} complete the cycle. Their art is drawn as handmade animalfolk art showing the seasons the Calamities bring, and the art of the red one shows Lily against Wildsear.",
                        "The Class enchantment, first seen in Adventures in the Forgotten Realms, came to a true Magic world for the first time as the Talents, cards for the jobs of Valley, innkeepers, blacksmiths, artists and more. A Class starts with one ability and can be levelled up at sorcery speed to unlock more. The rare cycle is {Caretaker's Talent}, {Stormchaser's Talent}, {Scavenger's Talent}, {Artist's Talent} and {Innkeeper's Talent}, whose third level doubles every counter you would place. The uncommon cycle is {Builder's Talent}, {Gossip's Talent}, {Bandit's Talent}, {Blacksmith's Talent} and {Hunter's Talent}, and the Commander decks added four more. The set also changed templating for good. Enters the battlefield became simply enters, and as long as it's your turn became during your turn.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Story", "Bloomburrow Mechanics, by Matt Tabak, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Bloomburrow Release Notes, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-cards", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-the-big-beasts", "The Big Beasts",
                    listOf(
                        "The mythic Calamities are the set's showpieces and each plays very differently. {Maha, Its Feathers Night} is a five mana flying trampler whose ward makes an opponent discard to target it, and it sets the base toughness of every opposing creature to 1, so a single damage spell or a little combat wipes out boards. {Ygra, Eater of All} turns every other creature into a Food artifact as well and grows two +1/+1 counters every time a Food hits the graveyard, which means every creature that dies feeds it. {Lumra, Bellow of the Woods} is as big as your land count, mills four as it enters and returns every land in your graveyard to play.",
                        "{Beza, the Bounding Spring} checks whether any opponent is ahead of you in lands, life, creatures or cards in hand and catches you up in each, with a Treasure, four life, two Fish or a card. {Eluge, the Shoreless Sea} floods lands into Islands and makes your first instant or sorcery each turn cheaper for each flooded land. {Dragonhawk, Fate's Tempest} exiles cards to play for every big creature you control and burns opponents for whatever goes unplayed. None of them needs an animal theme around it, which makes all six, Wildsear included, natural picks for a Commander deck built around the beast itself.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-legends-to-build", "Legends to Build",
                    listOf(
                        "Nearly every animal has a legend to lead it. {Mabel, Heir to Cragflame} pumps your Mice and brings her sword. {Valley Questcaller} lifts rabbits, bats, birds and mice all at once. {Wick, the Whorled Mind} makes snails for every rat and turns a big snail into damage and cards. {Vren, the Relentless} exiles every opposing creature that would die and hands you a rat for each. {Camellia, the Seedmiser} turns Food into squirrels. {Kastral, the Windcrested} drops birds from your hand or graveyard whenever birds connect. {Zoraline, Cosmos Caller} gains life for every attacking bat and pays life to bring back cheap permanents. {Clement, the Worrywort} bounces your own creatures to replay them and lets frogs tap for mana. {Gev, Scaled Scorch} pings an opponent for every Lizard you cast.",
                        "The three colour legends are built for Commander. {Helga, Skittish Seer} ramps into large creatures and draws a card as you cast each one. {Glarb, Calamity's Augur} lets you play lands and big spells off the top of your library. {Hugs, Grisly Guardian} digs deep with an X spell and gives you an extra land drop every turn. {Baylen, the Haymaker} taps tokens for mana, cards and counters. {Alania, Divergent Storm} copies spells in exchange for letting an opponent draw a card. {Flubs, the Fool}, the Buy a Box promo, plays an extra land and draws or discards with every spell. And {Ral, Crackling Wit} anchors any blue and red spells deck.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "No battle is fought, or won, alone.",
                        "Valley Questcaller",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-treatments-and-guests", "Treatments and Guests",
                    listOf(
                        "Bloomburrow came in a great many costumes. The basic lands are full art Seasonal lands, every type drawn in spring, summer, autumn and winter, with spring the most common in boosters and winter the rarest. The Calamity Beasts come in the borderless Fieldnotes showcase, and a second showcase frame called Woodland dresses up the legends and many other cards. The Seasons come borderless, {Three Tree City} has four borderless versions, one for each season, and a run of borderless cards came in raised foil with anime style art. Extended art rares fill out the Collector Boosters. Bloomburrow was also the last set printed in Simplified Chinese.",
                        "The Imagine Courageous Critters showcase asks what famous characters would look like as animals of Valley, though none of these visits are part of the story. [Jace](who/jace) is a fox on {Jace, the Mind Sculptor}, [Liliana](who/liliana) a squirrel, [Teferi](who/teferi) a secretarybird, Nissa a frog and Narset a lapwing, and [Karn](who/karn) becomes an iron ash tree in the Forest of Iron on {Karn, the Great Creator}. Ten Special Guests cards rode along in Play Boosters too, from {Swords to Plowshares} and {Sword of Fire and Ice} to {Ledger Shredder}, {Frogmite}, described in its flavor as an otterfolk inventor's failed frog diving suit, and {Toski, Bearer of Secrets}. Starting here, The List in Play Boosters held only Special Guests.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "It started as a prototype for a frog diving suit, but the internal mechanisms didn't leave room for the frog.",
                        "Fitzwhisker, otterfolk inventor, Frogmite",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow Commander"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Imagine: Courageous Critters"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Collecting Bloomburrow, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "blb-commander", "Commander Decks",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-animated-army", "Animated Army",
                    listOf(
                        "Four Commander decks came out beside the set, under the code BLC, each with new legends, a pair of commanders, a new Talent and a planeswalker reprinted with Imagine art. Animated Army is red and green, and its pitch is make trash and do smash. Its face is {Bello, Bard of the Brambles}, a raccoon bard known as the ringtail bard, the trinketmaster and lately the omentrotter. He has walked through the Omenpaths to other worlds and brought back souvenirs, Thran artifacts from Dominaria, crystals from Ikoria, filigree gears from Ghirapur, and his proudest treasure, a hedron from the highest point of the Akoum Skyclave.",
                        "Bello's magic wakes the spirit in every object, some gentle as a hearth's spark and some as wild as an avalanche, and the card does just that. On your turn each artifact and enchantment you control with mana value four or more becomes a 4/4 indestructible Elemental with haste that draws a card when it hits a player. The other commander is {Wildsear, Scouring Maw}, the old wildfire wolf itself, which gives cascade to enchantments you cast from your hand. The deck's new Talent is {Alchemist's Talent}. Bello himself has called Wildsear a shadow of its past self, still more than a match for Valley, and wondered aloud where the power Lily took from it went.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Back in the age of Lily, Wildsear was known as the Season of Flames. Its flames would burn forever. That was until Lily took the Seedflame from them.",
                        "Bello, bard of the Brambles, The Legends of Bloomburrow",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-family-matters", "Family Matters",
                    listOf(
                        "Family Matters is white, blue and red, and its pitch is make offspring and go wide. Its face is {Zinnia, Valley's Voice}, a nomadic stork bard who crashed into Three Tree City, quite literally, and has been playing the lute there since. Nobody knows where Zinnia came from, and rumour says a noble house of birdfolk far away. When the Storm Hawk arrived, Zinnia showed the flying and fighting skill of a veteran Plumecreed soldier and led the rescue of a border village, outracing the beast. Folk now call Zinnia the Sovereign of Life, and it is good luck to have them sing at a wedding.",
                        "On the card Zinnia gives offspring to every creature you cast and grows for each other creature you control with base power 1, which is every offspring token there is. The other commander is {Arthur, Marigold Knight}, a mouse on a wicker steed whose many songs of heroism, some by Zinnia, nobody else ever witnessed, including the sword lesson he says he gave Mabel, which Mabel found very amusing. Arthur pulls a creature from the top of your library into each attack. The deck's Talent is {Fortune Teller's Talent}, and its planeswalker is {Elspeth, Sun's Champion}, drawn as a mouse.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-peace-offering", "Peace Offering",
                    listOf(
                        "Peace Offering is green, white and blue, a group hug deck that wins friends and builds +1/+1 counters until the table crumbles under its kindness. Its face is {Ms. Bumbleflower}, the gracious rabbit who runs the most popular inn in Three Tree City and its famous never ending stew, a giant cauldron always on the hearth that guests may take from and add to as they like. She inherited the inn from her mother, a retired pirate, and her mother's burly old friends still drop by to help and tell stories. She is the narrator of the Planeswalker's Guide history lesson, ladling out stew to a newly arrived traveller.",
                        "On the card, every spell you cast lets an opponent draw a card while you put a counter on a creature and give it flight, and the second time it happens in a turn you draw two. The other commander is {Mr. Foxglove}, a dashing fox swashbuckler said to be the adventurer most likely to win your heart, who once stumbled into her inn half dead and has been finding excuses to visit at odd hours ever since. She has not noticed he is courting her. He draws cards to match the defending player's hand. The deck's Talent is {Fisher's Talent} and its planeswalker is {Tamiyo, Field Researcher}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Secret ingredient? What do you think?",
                        "Ms. Bumbleflower, Carrot Cake",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "blb-squirreled-away", "Squirreled Away",
                    listOf(
                        "Squirreled Away is black and green, a deck that hoards tokens and spends them. Its face is {Hazel of the Rootbloom}, the cachemaster whose name is whispered to frighten misbehaving children. She leads the Belladonna, a vicious, secretive faction of squirrels who want to take the plane back for nature. She began as a humble cacheworker on a farming commune, grew furious at its waste and its treatment of the land, and took control after its old leader died of something that looked a great deal like nightshade. She once bridled the Calamity of Spring itself and used its power to bury a whole village under berry bushes as tall as trees.",
                        "Those who know her say she is secretly soft and a fantastic boss. Her card taps tokens for mana and copies a token at every end step, two copies if it is a squirrel. The other commander is {The Odd Acorn Gang}, the trio she calls when something needs doing, brawny Rosemary, prankish Oregano and silent Thyme with her bow, who can barely stand one another off the job but are deadly on it. The deck's Talent is {Gourmand's Talent} and its planeswalker is {Garruk, Cursed Huntsman}, drawn as a honey badger.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "May all your caches be full.",
                        "Hazel of the Rootbloom",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow Commander"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Bloomburrow, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Bloomburrow Commander decks"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
